title: 'pallet-revive: record no storage deposit for writes under Root'
doc:
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    Storage written by a call stack whose origin is `Root` no longer adds to the deposit a
    contract records.

    Before this change the pallet priced such a write at `DepositPerByte` and
    `DepositPerChildTrieItem` and added it to `ContractInfo`. Settlement then collected nothing,
    because Root has no account to charge, so the contract recorded more deposit than it held.

    On a runtime that leaves `Config::Deposit` at the default `()`, a refund computed from the
    inflated record paid out of deposit other users had put down. Once the hold ran short, a
    refund failed with `StorageRefundNotEnoughFunds`. Under `PGasDeposit` a user who had
    contributed to the contract could draw the excess out of the hold, down to and including the
    base deposit. Both are fixed. For storage written after the upgrade a contract never records
    more deposit than it holds.

    Refunds change for everyone holding storage in a contract Root writes to. The pallet refunds
    a removal pro rata of the deposit the contract holds, over every unit it holds, and Root's
    units now count toward that total without having added to the pool. A depositor who paid for
    `a` units of a contract that also holds `r` Root-written units gets back `a / (a + r)` of
    what they paid. The remainder stays held on the contract until it is terminated. Before this
    change the same depositor got back everything they had paid, because a Root write added to
    the pool at list price as well as to the count.

    Whoever removes Root-written units is paid a pro rata share of a pool they did not fund.
    Where one depositor paid 132 for two slots and Root then wrote fifty, an account that clears
    all fifty is credited 126 of that 132. Under `()` it keeps the 126. Under `PGasDeposit` the
    native part of a refund is capped at that account's own `NativeDepositOf` contribution, which
    is nothing here, so a deposit paid in native stays held on the contract. A deposit paid in
    PGAS has no such cap, so the account receives `RefundPercent` of the 126 and the rest is
    burned.

    The pallet pays nobody for storage that Root removes. Clearing the fifty slots Root wrote
    leaves the depositor's whole 132 in the pool, so the dilution those writes caused is undone.
    The pallet does that by restoring the pool to what the surviving units would cost at the
    current `DepositPerByte` and `DepositPerChildTrieItem`. If either price has fallen since the
    depositor paid, that is less than they paid, and the shortfall stays held on the contract
    without being recorded. Clearing a contract's last slots empties the pool instead.

    Deposit that leaves the record without being paid out stays held on the contract, and Root
    removing storage a signed origin paid for strands it the same way. Whoever terminates the
    contract receives the whole hold. When Root terminates it, the pallet account does.
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    There is no `Config` change and no storage migration. `ContractInfo` keeps its layout, and
    recorded deposits stay valid.

    This release does not repair contracts that are already in the inflated state. A chain that
    has executed Root-originated storage writes keeps those contracts recording more deposit than
    they hold, and their refunds keep failing or over-drawing as before. The pallet accounts
    correctly only for storage written after the upgrade.

    Contracts instantiated by a Root call stack record a base deposit of zero and hold nothing. A
    later signed write to such a contract is charged and recorded in full.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: minor
